import{_ as t,o as a,c as o,a7 as i}from"./chunks/framework.DUCm5Bu5.js";const g=JSON.parse('{"title":"Store management","description":"Browse store catalogs, manage sources and stage server packages through the administration interface.","frontmatter":{"title":"Store management","description":"Browse store catalogs, manage sources and stage server packages through the administration interface.","updated":"2026-10-25T00:00:00.000Z"},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/guides/store.md","filePath":"en/guides/store.md"}'),r={name:"en/guides/store.md"};function s(n,e,d,c,l,h){return a(),o("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[i('<h1 id="store-management" tabindex="-1">Store management <a class="header-anchor" href="#store-management" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Store management {#store-management}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>The store is the administration interface for distributing server packages. It browses configured catalog sources, previews GitHub release metadata, and downloads server packages into a verified staging area. Everything on this page is protected by an administrator session: sign in through the WebUI (or <code>POST /api/auth/login</code>), and every store request requires that session cookie. The store <strong>stages files and nothing else</strong>: no archive is extracted, no plugin is loaded, and no application path is replaced by any store action. A completed download is an inventory of verified files, not an installed plugin.</p><p>The store ships in the core bundle (rows <code>distribution.sources</code>, <code>distribution.plans</code>, <code>distribution.packages</code> and <code>distribution.jobs</code>), which the <code>kr-812</code>, <code>management</code> and <code>all</code> profiles include. Staging lands under <code>data/distribution-staging</code> by default, held by an exclusive owner lock; history pages default to 20 entries; the free-space reserve defaults to zero. Sources are stored in <code>data/distribution-sources.json</code> — setting <code>state_path</code> empty makes the source list read-only.</p><h2 id="sources" tabindex="-1">Sources <a class="header-anchor" href="#sources" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Sources {#sources}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The Sources card lists the configured catalog sources in operator order, and the order matters: the first source that serves a catalog is selected by default. Each row has an id, a display name, a kind (<code>http</code>, <code>alist</code> or <code>github</code>), a root URL and a catalog path. URLs carry no credentials, query or fragment; the root must end in <code>/</code>. Move rows up and down, add and remove them, then save the whole list at once.</p><p>Saving uses an optimistic revision: the editor captures the settings revision when it opens and sends it back with the save. If another administrator saved in between, the save is refused with <code>source_revision_conflict</code> and the dialog stays open for you to review and reapply.</p><h2 id="github-mirror" tabindex="-1">GitHub mirror <a class="header-anchor" href="#github-mirror" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;GitHub mirror {#github-mirror}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Where GitHub is slow or blocked, the store can download GitHub release attachments and archives through public URL-prefix mirrors. Open <strong>Manage sources</strong> and turn on <strong>GitHub mirror acceleration</strong>; <strong>Automatic</strong> measures every mirror before each download and tries the fastest available ones first, or choose one mirror after a <strong>Speed test</strong>. GitHub itself is always the last route. Only payloads travel through a mirror: release information and catalogs are read from GitHub directly, and every mirrored byte is checked against the size and SHA-256 that GitHub published, so a mirror can make a download fail but cannot change what is installed.</p><p>The server checks its own address once. When it is in mainland China, the first administrator to open the WebUI is asked whether to turn the mirror on; the dialog is shown once and nothing changes until it is confirmed. The mirror list, the probe file and the measurement bounds are in <code>data/github-mirrors.json</code> (<code>github_mirrors_path</code> on the <code>distribution.sources</code> row; empty turns the feature off), and the switch, selection and location check are kept in <code>github_mirror_state_path</code>.</p><h2 id="browsing" tabindex="-1">Browsing the catalog <a class="header-anchor" href="#browsing" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Browsing the catalog {#browsing}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The catalog page shows one merged card list across all sources, keyed by package identity. A filter selects all sources or one; a refresh re-reads every catalog (or one source&#39;s). A source that fails is never dropped silently — its failure (<code>source_timeout</code>, <code>source_unavailable</code> or <code>catalog_invalid</code>) is listed above the cards while healthy sources still render.</p><p>When several releases of the same package appear in one catalog, the highest publisher <code>revision</code> wins; a tie — or a multi-release group whose revisions are unset — is a configuration error rather than a guess. Client-targeted packages are visible but download-only for server targets: selecting one shows &quot;Client plugins are available for preview only&quot;.</p><h2 id="github-repositories" tabindex="-1">GitHub repositories <a class="header-anchor" href="#github-repositories" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;GitHub repositories {#github-repositories}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A GitHub source reads catalog documents from the repository. The Repository release form previews a repository&#39;s latest stable release — or an explicitly selected tag — with its attachments: drafts and prereleases are refused, every asset URL must belong to the requested repository and tag, and an attachment carries a publisher SHA-256 only when GitHub publishes a <code>sha256:</code> digest for it. The preview is metadata only; nothing is downloaded by it.</p><p>The <code>github_repository</code> and <code>github_tag</code> fields are catalog schema 3. They work against the server and the Launcher&#39;s strict catalog readers, but they are a provisional coordination format, not a published shared contract — do not republish production catalogs to schema 3 without agreeing the format with Launcher releases first. A repository attachment can be downloaded only when it carries that publisher digest; the download button stays disabled otherwise, because the byte-for-byte verification a download performs has nothing to compare against.</p><h2 id="staging" tabindex="-1">Downloading to staging <a class="header-anchor" href="#staging" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Downloading to staging {#staging}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Downloading a server package is a three-step flow, and each step pins exactly what was shown:</p><ol><li><strong>Select</strong> a download offer on the card. Each offer carries a selection fingerprint — a SHA-256 over the configured source and the exact offer — and the SHA-256 of the exact catalog bytes it was read from.</li><li><strong>Plan</strong> the download. The plan revalidates the source, catalog, release and attachment against those pins; a changed source or offer is refused with <code>distribution_source_changed</code> or <code>distribution_selection_changed</code> instead of downloading something else.</li><li><strong>Admit</strong> the job. The job verifies every file&#39;s size and SHA-256 as it streams, keeps the blobs opaque under the staging root, and reports phases (<code>preparing</code>, <code>transferring</code>, <code>staged</code>). The Downloads panel polls progress, verifies a staged job&#39;s full readback on demand, cancels active work and deletes terminal jobs with their staging.</li></ol><p>A passing verification states what the staging volume held at that instant. It grants no installation authority, and a later change on disk is detected by verifying again, not trusted forward.</p><h2 id="client-plugin-policy" tabindex="-1">Client policy <a class="header-anchor" href="#client-plugin-policy" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Client policy {#client-plugin-policy}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The client policy that decides which plugins Launchers must, may or must not use is edited in the WebUI of an installed server. Saving requires the <code>webui.settings.client_policy</code> permission on top of the administrator session and reruns the configuration application. These rules configure compatible Launchers; client binaries are not installed on the server, and the rules prove nothing about client integrity. See <a href="./plugins#client-plugin-policy">Client policy</a> for the management API and <a href="./launcher#client-policy">the rule types</a>.</p><h2 id="administration-api" tabindex="-1">Administration API <a class="header-anchor" href="#administration-api" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Administration API {#administration-api}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The WebUI calls the same endpoints an operator script would. All paths below require the administrator session cookie; non-GET requests also require the <code>X-BFF-Request: 1</code> header with a matching same-origin <code>Origin</code>.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Method and path</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>GET/PUT /api/distribution/v1/sources</code></td><td>Read or replace the source list with its revision.</td></tr><tr><td><code>POST /api/distribution/v1/repository-release</code></td><td>Preview one GitHub release (metadata only).</td></tr><tr><td><code>GET /api/distribution/v1/catalog?sourceId=</code></td><td>The merged catalog snapshot, or one source&#39;s.</td></tr><tr><td><code>POST /api/distribution/v1/plans</code> · <code>/validate</code> · <code>DELETE</code></td><td>Pin, recheck and release a selection.</td></tr><tr><td><code>POST /api/distribution/v1/plans/{id}/files</code></td><td>Verify the manifest and list the payload files.</td></tr><tr><td><code>GET/POST /api/distribution/v1/jobs</code> · <code>GET /{id}</code></td><td>History page and admission (202 with a <code>Location</code> header).</td></tr><tr><td><code>POST /api/distribution/v1/jobs/{id}/cancel</code> · <code>/verify</code> · <code>DELETE</code></td><td>Cancel, verify the staged readback, or remove a terminal job.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Stable error codes (<code>source_revision_conflict</code>, <code>distribution_selection_changed</code>, <code>distribution_job_not_staged</code>, <code>repository_rate_limited</code>, and the rest) name the failure; the HTTP status distinguishes a bad request (400), a stale pin (409), an upstream problem (502/504) and a storage or configuration problem (503).</p><h2 id="limits" tabindex="-1">What the store does not do <a class="header-anchor" href="#limits" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What the store does not do {#limits}&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>No installation or activation: no route extracts, loads or restarts anything. Downloading is not installing, and a durable intent is not a running feature.</li><li>GitHub mirrors fail by design instead of pretending to work.</li><li>Cross-origin manifest references are refused (&quot;not supported yet&quot;).</li><li>The Launcher consumes none of this yet: it does not read store catalogs, and client presets are advertised data until its download engine ships.</li></ul>',27)])])}const p=t(r,[["render",s]]);export{g as __pageData,p as default};
